---
export const prerender = true;
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { ORG } from '../lib/org';
---

<Content
  kicker="Policy"
  title="Prohibited Services"
  description={`What creators may not claim, promise, or sell through ${ORG.name}, applied the same way across every religious and spiritual tradition offered on the platform.`}
  lead="The claims, practices, and services we do not allow — one standard, applied the same way to every tradition."
  updated="2026-09-20"
>
  <p>
    {ORG.name} hosts paid live streaming and paid one-to-one consultations in a defined set of
    categories: puja and darshan, temple tours, satsang and religious sermons, and festival
    broadcasts as live events; and astrology, palmistry, tarot reading, numerology, kundli
    matching, vastu, pandit consultation, and meditation as one-to-one consultations. This page
    sets out what a listing in any of these categories may not do. It applies the same way to
    every religious tradition, spiritual practice, and belief system offered on the platform.
    None is exempt, and none is treated as more or less legitimate than another — every listing is
    judged only against the rules below.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Guaranteed outcomes</h2>
  <p>
    A listing may not promise a specific real-world result as a guaranteed or assured consequence
    of paying for the service. This includes guaranteeing: wealth or financial gain; marriage or a
    specific relationship outcome; conception or the birth of a child; success in an examination;
    or approval of a visa or immigration application. A listing may describe the guidance,
    reading, or ritual on offer, and may describe what it is traditionally intended to achieve,
    but it may not state or imply that any of these outcomes is certain, guaranteed, or assured.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Medical claims</h2>
  <p>
    A listing may not claim to cure, treat, diagnose, or prevent any physical or mental illness,
    disease, disability, or medical condition, including infertility. A listing may not advise a
    customer to delay, reduce, or stop medical treatment, medication, or a doctor's advice in
    favor of the service being offered.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Fear-based sales practices</h2>
  <p>
    A listing may not tell a customer, or imply, that they or their family are currently
    suffering harm from a curse, evil eye, black magic, planetary affliction, ancestral
    displeasure, or any other unseen force, and then offer a paid service to remove or reverse
    it. A listing may not claim that a specific danger is imminent in a customer's chart,
    horoscope, or reading. A listing may not require a purchase within a short deadline — for
    example, "within 24 hours" — to avert a claimed danger. A listing may not price a remedy
    according to how afraid, wealthy, or desperate a customer appears to be, rather than at a
    fixed, published price.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Harm</h2>
  <p>
    A listing may not involve killing, injuring, or sacrificing an animal, living or otherwise,
    as part of the service performed or described. A listing may not involve fire, flame, or
    chemical substances used in a way that creates a foreseeable risk of injury or property
    damage to the customer or anyone else. No minor may participate as a paid party to any
    listing — as the person performing the service, the person being read or consulted, or in
    any other capacity connected to the transaction.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Pressure tactics</h2>
  <p>
    A listing, and the session delivered under it, may not use high-pressure sales tactics to
    convert a browsing customer into a paying one — for example, escalating claims of urgency or
    danger mid-conversation, refusing to answer a direct question about price, or continuing to
    solicit payment after a customer has declined.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Who actually performs the service</h2>
  <p>
    Every listing must accurately state who performs the service. Where the person publishing the
    listing is not the person performing the ritual, reading, or ceremony, the listing must name
    who does perform it, name anyone who facilitates the session on the creator's behalf, and name
    the temple or site where the event takes place, if it takes place at one. A listing that
    misrepresents who performs the service is treated the same as a listing that violates any
    other rule on this page.
  </p>

  <h2>7. One standard for every tradition</h2>
  <p>
    These rules describe conduct, not belief. They apply equally to every religious, spiritual,
    and cultural tradition available on {ORG.name}. We do not treat any tradition as more valid,
    more legitimate, or safer than another, and we do not use language on this page, in reviews,
    or in enforcement to characterize any tradition or its practices. A listing is judged only
    against the rules above, whichever tradition it belongs to.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Enforcement</h2>
  <p>
    These rules are enforced automatically when a creator submits a listing for review, and again
    before it is published — a listing that fails any rule above cannot go live. Listings are also
    reviewed after publication, and one that is found to violate this page is removed, whether or
    not it was caught at submission. Repeated violations, or a single violation involving a
    guaranteed medical claim or a threat tied to a purchase, can result in removal of the
    creator's account.
  </p>

  <h2>9. Reporting a listing</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone can report a listing that appears to break these rules from inside the app, or by
    emailing {ORG.email.replace('@', ' (@) ')}. Include a link to the listing, or
    the creator's handle, and what you saw. We review reports and act where a violation is
    confirmed.
  </p>

  <p>
    See also our <a href="/acceptable-use">Acceptable Use Policy</a> and
    <a href="/terms">Terms of Service</a>, which apply alongside this page to
    everything on {ORG.name}.
  </p>
</Content>
